"""
serial_server: the simcard-server HTTP API, backed by the SIM in a USB serial
(AT command) modem that ModemManager has not claimed, e.g. a phone's ACM port.

Same API as server.py (USIM-https-server compatible, plain HTTP):

  GET /?type=imsi
      -> {"imsi": "311480..."}

  GET /?type=rand-autn&rand=<32 hex>&autn=<32 hex>[&app=isim]
      -> {"res": "<hex>", "ck": "<hex>", "ik": "<hex>"}
      app=isim (an extension, for IMS AKA) runs it on the ISIM, or on the
      USIM if the card has no ISIM.
      On AKA synchronisation failure (AUTS in "res", as SWu-IKEv2 expects):
      -> {"res": "<auts>", "ck": null, "ik": null, "auts": "<28 hex>"}

  GET /?type=apdu&hex=<apdu hex>
      Raw APDU on the USIM (CLA channel bits are set for a logical channel).
      -> {"data": "<hex>", "sw": "9000"}

  GET /?type=imei
      The modem's IMEI (AT+CGSN), an extension for mkconfig.
      -> {"imei": "35..."}

  GET /?type=msisdn
      The SIM's own number (AT+CNUM), else the one given with --msisdn, an
      extension for mkconfig. 404 when there is neither.
      -> {"msisdn": "+1..."}

  GET /?type=isim
      The ISIM's identities (TS 31.103), an extension for mkconfig.
      -> {"impi": "...", "domain": "...", "impu": ["sip:...", "tel:..."]}

APDUs go over a logical channel of our own, next to the modem's basic
channel: opened with AT+CCHO/AT+CGLA (TS 27.007 8.45/8.43) when the modem has
them, else with MANAGE CHANNEL sent through AT+CSIM (Samsung phones). As a
last resort they go over AT+CSIM on the basic channel, re-selecting the USIM
before every operation since the modem moves that channel around too.

    python3 serial_server.py --port /dev/ttyACM0
    python3 serial_server.py --port /dev/serial/by-id/usb-...-if01 --mode csim
    python3 serial_server.py --port /dev/ttyACM0 --probe   # IMSI, then exit

Samsung phones only allow it while the screen is unlocked: the server waits
for an unlock at startup, and requests made while locked fail with a
"phone is locked" error. Developer options > Stay awake keeps it usable.

Needs pyserial and read/write access to the port (root or the tty's group).
The port must not belong to ModemManager: both would talk AT on it at once.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import atexit
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
